WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:05.008
Before you trust a treasury or an issuer, ask who can actually sign for it.

2
00:00:05.008 --> 00:00:07.420
This video shows how to count it properly.

3
00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:11.686
There are three ways to sign for an account.

4
00:00:11.686 --> 00:00:14.603
The master key is the account's original key pair.

5
00:00:15.053 --> 00:00:20.642
A regular key is a second key the account nominates, which lets the master key stay offline.

6
00:00:21.092 --> 00:00:25.024
And a signer list names up to thirty-two signers, each with a weight.

7
00:00:25.024 --> 00:00:29.924
A transaction is valid when the weights of the people who signed add up to the quorum.

8
00:00:31.624 --> 00:00:34.038
Here is a treasury with five signers.

9
00:00:34.038 --> 00:00:38.214
Alice has weight three, Bob two, Carol two, Dan one and Eve one.

10
00:00:38.214 --> 00:00:39.453
The quorum is five.

11
00:00:39.903 --> 00:00:46.680
Alice and Bob together reach five, so only two people are needed, not three of five as the headcount suggests.

12
00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:50.314
NOSHASHI's Control Surface works this out from the weights.

13
00:00:52.014 --> 00:00:58.743
And if the master key is still enabled, one person holding it can sign alone and skip the whole signer list.

14
00:00:58.743 --> 00:01:01.422
NOSHASHI flags that as a master-key bypass.

15
00:01:03.122 --> 00:01:11.954
An account whose master key is disabled, whose regular key points at an address nobody can sign for, and which has no signer list, is blackholed.

16
00:01:12.404 --> 00:01:14.580
Nobody can ever sign for it again.

17
00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:19.252
Its settings and its supply are frozen in time, for better and for worse.
